Context (pp. 36-38): Grothendieck is building the combinatorial category that indexes the boundary strata of the moduli spaces M_{g,ν} of stable curves. Objects are dual graphs of stable curves ('MD-graphs', Mumford–Deligne). Arrows are the 'specialization/generalization' morphisms, which in modern terms are edge contractions.

Graph formalism used. A graph G has:
- a vertex set S;
- a set Ã of oriented arcs (half-edges / oriented edges);
- a fixed-point-free involution σ_Ã (reversal of orientation);
- an origin map o: Ã → S.
On the disjoint union S ⊔ Ã he extends these structures:
- σ_G = σ_Ã on Ã and the identity on S;
- 'origine' = o on Ã and the identity on S.

Definition (44). A generalized morphism G' → G'' is a map φ: S' ⊔ Ã' → S'' ⊔ Ã'' such that:
- φ(S') ⊂ S'';
- φ may send arcs to vertices (these arcs are the contracted ones);
- φ commutes with σ_G and with the extended origin map.

Equivalent data:
1. a map f_S: S' → S'';
2. a σ-stable subset B̃' ⊂ Ã' (the arcs that are not contracted);
3. a map B̃' → Ã'' commuting with σ and compatible with the origin maps (via f_S).
Condition (45), added in the margin: for every arc a ∈ Ã' \ B̃', the origin and the extremity of a (i.e. o(a) and o(σa)) have the same image under f_S. In other words, a contracted edge collapses onto a single vertex.

A third reformulation: a subgraph G_{B̃'} of G' with the same vertices and arc set B̃', together with an ordinary graph morphism G_{B̃'} → G''.

Composition is evident (next page), giving the 'category of graphs with generalized morphisms', whose isomorphisms are the ordinary ones. On the next page, MD-graph structure is added: genus g: S → N, legs I → S, and a bijection on legs I' ≅ I''. Further conditions (presumably genus additivity and surjectivity) then cut this down to genuine contraction (specialization) morphisms of stable graphs.

What it serves: making the stratification of the Deligne–Mumford boundary functorial. Strata closures correspond to graph contractions, and the stratification is indexed by this category.
